  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x)$ and the given matrix $E,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi E)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $2$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi x).$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi E)=[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi ) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi ) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos(\\pi E) & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi J))\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
